/*****************************************************************************
andProbe -- the AND truth table, and the conjunction half of the operand
truthiness contract. Sibling to orProbe, which it deliberately mirrors.

Built 2026-08-11 during the AND OR rung and KEPT rather than discarded,
because it earned its place before a line of the rung was written. It was
made to answer one question and it caught a contradiction between a
normative table and measured behaviour, which is the cheapest kind of
finding there is.

WHAT EACH SECTION IS FOR, and none of them is decoration.

Section 1 is the truth table for the word form. These three rows did not
move across the rung and that is the claim -- the promotion changed WHEN
the arms run, never WHAT the conjunction answers.

Section 2 is PINNED WRONG ON PURPOSE. The symbol form answers false for
true and true, which is not a truth table at all. The cause is structural
and pointable rather than inferred: the symbol is registered bare in
incant setup, with no operateMethod, exactly the state the vertical bar
pair was in before it was filled on 2026-08-01. So the symbol matches an
inert operator and fails the body. Filed as a known error with its own
repair rung, deliberately NOT fixed here, because widening the rung to
the symbol forms is a ruling and not a rung.

Sections 3 and 3b are the short circuit rows, and they are a PAIR for a
reason. The word form skips its right arm and prints nothing. The symbol
form still evaluates it and prints the marker. Two spellings of one
operator with different evaluation semantics is a trap in waiting, and
these two lines sitting next to each other are the record of it.

Section 3c fires a CALL in left position. Rows 1 through 3b all use bare
fields on the left, so none of them touches the arm firing path at all --
a fixture can look thorough and still leave the interesting branch cold.

Section 4 is the assignment position, and it recorded a PREDICTION THAT
FAILED and has since been DISCHARGED. Before the AND OR rung, a false
conjunction returned no node at all, so the receiving field held no data
and read as false. After the rung it holds the number zero, and the
prediction was that this would not change how the field reads. It DID
change -- the field read TRUE -- because a bare field test went by
presence and not by value. ⚠ bareIfTruth (2026-09-08, Tony's ruling)
closed that: aCTionIF now answers by truthOf, so a field holding zero
reads FALSE and this row moved TRUE -> false. The only shipping consumer
of the shape is displayIfVisible in incant utilities, defined and never
called, so nothing live moved in either direction.

Section 5 is why, and it is now the RECORD of a closed gap rather than an
open one. A field holding zero dumps as an int carrying zero; the bare
test read TRUE while the same field through the word form read false. The
bare test and the operator DISAGREED, and the ruled contract governed the
operator only. ⚠ This file's own header named the exit -- "closing that
gap is a separate ruling with its own customer" -- and bareIfTruth is that
customer. `if aFalse;` now reads false, matching AND. ⚠ `if !aFalse;`
still reads false and is a SEPARATE, PRE-EXISTING defect: it was measured
false on both arms of bareIfTruth's own control, so it did not move and is
not this ruling's doing. Filed rather than chased.

Section 6 is bareIfTruth's driver, and it is the row the ruling was
written for: `if length;` on a ZERO-LENGTH list must read FALSE. Its
non-zero sibling is not decoration -- a zero-expecting row alone cannot
tell "answered by value" from "answered by nothing", so the 3-member list
is the anti-vacuity control and both rows are pinned.

ZERO TEXT BEARING LOCALS, so this fixture stays inside the audited set
and the KE-4 length defect cannot masquerade in any row above.
*****************************************************************************/
Start();
include(unitTests);
search reset stack Grokking;
search UnitTests list;
register(AndProbe);
define
    aTrue = 1;
    aFalse = 0;
    apEmpty;
    apFull ap1 ap2 ap3;
    loudOne code={ print + "    [RIGHT ARM EVALUATED]":; return aTrue; };
    loudLeft code={ print + "    [LEFT ARM EVALUATED]":; return aTrue; };
    runAnd code={
        print "1 the WORD form, AND, on fields holding 1 and 0:":;
        if aTrue && aTrue;     print + "true  AND true  -> TRUE":;
        else                    print + "true  AND true  -> false":;
        if aTrue && aFalse;    print + "true  AND false -> TRUE":;
        else                    print + "true  AND false -> false":;
        if aFalse && aTrue;    print + "false AND true  -> TRUE":;
        else                    print + "false AND true  -> false":;
        print "2 the SYMBOL form, &&  -- PINNED WRONG, see the header:":;
        if aTrue && aTrue;      print + "true  && true  -> TRUE":;
        else                    print + "true  && true  -> false":;
        if aTrue && aFalse;     print + "true  && false -> TRUE":;
        else                    print + "true  && false -> false":;
        if aFalse && aTrue;     print + "false && true  -> TRUE":;
        else                    print + "false && true  -> false":;
        print "3 SHORT-CIRCUIT, the SYMBOL form, left arm FALSE:":;
        if aFalse && loudOne();   print + "false && loudOne() -> TRUE":;
        else                      print + "false && loudOne() -> false":;
        print "3b SHORT-CIRCUIT, the WORD form, left arm FALSE:":;
        if aFalse && loudOne();  print + "false AND loudOne() -> TRUE":;
        else                      print + "false AND loudOne() -> false":;
        print "3c A CALL IN LEFT POSITION -- the arm-firing path, left TRUE:":;
        if loudLeft() && loudOne();  print + "loudLeft() AND loudOne() -> TRUE":;
        else                          print + "loudLeft() AND loudOne() -> false":;
        print "4 ASSIGNMENT POSITION -- what the result LOOKS like:":;
        andOut = aTrue && aFalse;
        print + "andOut after (true AND false) =" andOut:;
        if andOut;              print + "  and if andOut; reads it TRUE":;
        else                    print + "  and if andOut; reads it false":;
        print "5 what `if` does with a 0-holding field -- NOT the same contract:":;
        if aFalse;              print + "  if aFalse; -> TRUE":;
        else                    print + "  if aFalse; -> false":;
        if aTrue;               print + "  if aTrue;  -> TRUE":;
        else                    print + "  if aTrue;  -> false":;
        if !aFalse;             print + "  if !aFalse; -> TRUE":;
        else                    print + "  if !aFalse; -> false":;
        print "6 bareIfTruth -- `if length;` by VALUE, with its non-zero sibling:":;
        apLen := apEmpty.listLengtH;
        if apLen;               print + "  if length; on an EMPTY list -> TRUE":;
        else                    print + "  if length; on an EMPTY list -> false":;
        apLen := apFull.listLengtH;
        if apLen;               print + "  if length; on a 3-MEMBER list -> TRUE":;
        else                    print + "  if length; on a 3-MEMBER list -> false":;
        };
    ;
runAnd();
print "AP SENTINEL":;
stop();
